🔷 What is Eureka?
Eureka is a **Service Registry** originally developed by Netflix.
In Spring, it is used via **Spring Cloud Netflix Eureka**.

Simple meaning:
A phone-book for microservices.
Services register their address, and other services look them up by **service name** (not hardcoded IP/port).

⚠️ Status note (interview-aware):
Netflix Eureka is in maintenance mode.
Still common in Spring Cloud interviews / older systems.
Modern alternatives: Kubernetes DNS/Service, Consul, Nacos.


🚀 Purpose of Eureka
In microservices, instances start/stop and change IPs often.
Eureka helps by:
1) Registering service instances
2) Letting other services discover them dynamically (Service Discovery)
3) Enabling client-side load balancing across instances


🧱 Core Components
| Component         | Role                                                                 |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Eureka Server** | Registry / directory of all service instances                        |
| **Eureka Client** | Microservice that registers itself and fetches registry to discover others |


⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)

1) Eureka Server starts
   - Central registry
   - Stores service-name → list of instance URLs

2) Eureka Client (each microservice) starts
   - Registers with Eureka using `spring.application.name`
   - Sends heartbeat (default ~30s) to stay alive in registry
   - Fetches registry cache to discover other services

3) Service Discovery + call
   - Caller uses logical name: `http://order-service/...`
   - Spring Cloud LoadBalancer picks one healthy instance
   - No hardcoded host/port needed


📦 Key Annotations / Setup
| Item                      | Purpose                                                                 |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `@EnableEurekaServer`     | Make this app a Eureka Server                                           |
| Eureka Client dependency  | `spring-cloud-starter-netflix-eureka-client` (auto-config registers it) |
| `@EnableDiscoveryClient`  | Optional/generic discovery annotation (modern style)                    |
| `@EnableEurekaClient`     | Older Eureka-specific annotation (often unnecessary now)                |
| `@LoadBalanced`           | Enables client-side LB on `RestTemplate` / `WebClient.Builder`          |

Modern tip:
Just adding the Eureka Client starter + `spring.application.name` is usually enough.
Prefer `@LoadBalanced WebClient` over older RestTemplate in new code.


📁 Example: Configuration

🖥️ application.yml for Eureka Server
```yaml
server:
  port: 8761

eureka:
  client:
    register-with-eureka: false   # server usually does not register itself
    fetch-registry: false         # standalone server does not fetch registry
```

🖥️ application.yml for Eureka Client
```yaml
spring:
  application:
    name: user-service            # this becomes the service ID in Eureka

eureka:
  client:
    service-url:
      defaultZone: http://localhost:8761/eureka/
```

✅ Discovery call example (logical service name)
```java
@LoadBalanced
@Bean
public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
    return new RestTemplate();
}

// "order-service" is resolved via Eureka + LoadBalancer (NOT DNS/hosts file)
String url = "http://order-service/api/orders";
ResponseEntity<Order[]> response = restTemplate.getForEntity(url, Order[].class);
```


🔁 Self-Preservation Mode (Important for Interviews)
If many heartbeats are missing (network blip / GC pause), Eureka does **not** aggressively remove instances.

Why?
To avoid wiping the registry during temporary network issues (AP-leaning behavior).

Disable only in local/test if you want fast deregistration:
```yaml
eureka:
  server:
    enable-self-preservation: false
```

Related lease settings (common interview terms):
- heartbeat / renew interval (~30s)
- eviction when renewals are missing (unless self-preservation holds them)


📉 Eureka vs Consul vs Zookeeper
| Feature              | Eureka        | Consul         | Zookeeper      |
| -------------------- | ------------- | -------------- | -------------- |
| Developed By         | Netflix       | HashiCorp      | Apache         |
| Service Discovery    | ✅             | ✅              | ✅              |
| Health Check         | Mostly client heartbeat | Strong server-side checks | Server-side |
| CAP tendency         | AP-ish        | More CP options| CP             |
| Built-in UI          | ✅             | ✅              | ❌              |
| Spring Cloud Support | ✅             | ✅              | Limited/older  |


❓ Interview Questions & Answers

Q1: What is Eureka in Spring Cloud?
A: A service registry so microservices register/discover each other by name instead of hardcoded IPs.

Q2: How does Eureka handle temporary network failures?
A: Self-preservation mode avoids mass eviction when heartbeats drop suddenly.

Q3: Eureka Server vs Eureka Client?
A: Server stores the registry; clients register, renew leases (heartbeat), and discover other services.

Q4: Default Eureka Server port?
A: 8761

Q5: Can Eureka be highly available?
A: Yes — run multiple Eureka servers in a peer-to-peer cluster (they replicate registrations).

What does peer-to-peer mean?
Peer-to-peer means there is **no single master Eureka server**. Every Eureka server is an equal peer.

```text
Eureka Server 1  ←→  Eureka Server 2  ←→  Eureka Server 3
       ↑                   ↑                   ↑
   services can register with any one; registries replicate to peers
```

| Idea              | Meaning                                                          |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Peer**          | Each Eureka server is equal (no primary/secondary boss)          |
| **Peer-to-peer**  | Servers talk to each other and copy registry data                |
| **Why**           | If one Eureka dies, others still have the registry (HA)          |

Contrast:
- Client–server: services talk to Eureka (normal registration)
- Peer-to-peer (among Eureka servers): Eureka servers sync with each other

Interview one-liner:
Eureka HA = multiple Eureka servers as peers that replicate registrations, so no single point of failure.

Q6: How does load balancing work with Eureka?
A: Client-side load balancing (Spring Cloud LoadBalancer). Caller asks Eureka for instances, then picks one locally.
(Ribbon is legacy/removed in modern Spring Cloud.)

Q7: Eureka vs Kubernetes service discovery?
A: In Kubernetes, Services/DNS often replace Eureka. Eureka is still useful in non-K8s VM/bare-metal Spring Cloud setups.


📺 Diagram: Simple Eureka Flow
```text
User
  ↓
API Gateway  ----discover---->  Eureka Server
  ↓                                ↑      ↑
User Service ----------------------+      |
  ↓ (call by name: order-service)         |
Order Service ----------------------------+

Flow:
1) User Service & Order Service REGISTER in Eureka
2) Gateway (or User Service) DISCOVERS instance list from Eureka
3) Request is forwarded to a chosen instance
```

(Older wrong idea to avoid: “User registers in Eureka”. Users never register — only services/instances do.)


